\details{
This function is used internally by the main biofetchR pipelines, but it is
also exported so users can apply the same cleaning and thinning logic to
occurrence tables before, after or outside a full pipeline run.

The function applies its checks in a fixed order:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Input standardisation}. A plain data frame is screened for usable
longitude/latitude values and converted to an \code{sf} object in EPSG:4326.
Existing \code{sf} inputs are retained as \code{sf} objects.
\item \strong{Coordinate screening}. Records with missing, non-finite, impossible
coordinates, or exact \verb{0,0} coordinates, are removed.
\item \strong{Coordinate-uncertainty handling}. If
\code{coordinateUncertaintyInMeters} is present, it is coerced to numeric. When
\code{filter_uncertain = TRUE}, records above \code{max_uncertainty_m} are removed.
When \code{filter_uncertain = FALSE}, those records are retained but uncertainty
still acts as a small priority penalty during thinning.
\item \strong{Priority ordering}. Records are sorted before thinning so that higher
values in \code{priority_col} are preferentially retained. By default,
\code{individualCount} is used when present.
\item \strong{Spatial thinning}. If \code{max_density} is supplied, density-based thinning
is applied. Otherwise, if \code{dist_km > 0}, greedy distance-based thinning is
applied using great-circle distances from \code{\link[geosphere:distHaversine]{geosphere::distHaversine()}}.
}

Set \code{dist_km = 0} and leave \code{max_density = NULL} to use the function as a
cleaning-only helper. In that mode no distance thinning is applied, and all
cleaned records are returned.
}
\section{Input requirements}{

\code{sf_obj} may be either:
\itemize{
\item an \code{sf} point object; or
\item a data frame/tibble with \code{decimalLongitude} and \code{decimalLatitude} columns.
}

Non-\code{sf} inputs are converted to \code{sf} with EPSG:4326 and with longitude and
latitude columns retained (\code{remove = FALSE}). Existing \code{sf} inputs are
assumed to represent point geometries with longitude/latitude-like
coordinates.
}

\section{Cleaning rules}{

The coordinate-cleaning step removes records with:
\itemize{
\item missing longitude or latitude;
\item non-finite longitude or latitude;
\item longitude outside \verb{[-180, 180]};
\item latitude outside \verb{[-90, 90]}; or
\item exact \verb{0,0} coordinates.
}

If \code{coordinateUncertaintyInMeters} is present and
\code{filter_uncertain = TRUE}, records with uncertainty greater than
\code{max_uncertainty_m} are also removed.
}

\section{Thinning behaviour}{

Distance-based thinning is greedy and order-dependent. The function therefore
sorts records before thinning so that higher-priority records are considered
first. If \code{coordinateUncertaintyInMeters} is available, higher uncertainty
reduces the thinning priority slightly, even when high-uncertainty records are
not filtered out.

Density-based thinning is activated by \code{max_density}. In that mode, the
function estimates the input bounding-box area and retains approximately
\code{max_density} records per km^2, after priority sorting.
}

\section{Output columns}{

The returned object is always an \code{sf} object. The function ensures that
\code{decimalLongitude} and \code{decimalLatitude} are available in the output. If
\code{return_all = TRUE}, a logical \code{.thinned} column indicates which rows were
retained by the thinning step. If \code{coordinateUncertaintyInMeters} is present,
a logical \code{.high_uncertainty} column flags retained records above
\code{warn_uncertainty_m}.
}

\section{Workflow note}{

This function is intentionally conservative and local-only. It does not
perform taxonomic cleaning, native-range filtering, GRIIS filtering, GBIF
download submission or environmental overlay extraction. Those steps belong
to higher-level biofetchR pipeline functions.
}

